--- body ---
## Context
The author asked to search his library scoped by **year**, and named
**author**, **title** and **doctype** alongside it. Two measurements already
bear on this and they point opposite ways, so the first job of this ticket is
to keep them apart.

**X4 does not block this.** X4 measured one mechanism — an arbitrary rowid set
shipped through `json_each` — and found it dominated by not scoping at all. That
is the mechanism a *collection* or a *tag* needs, because their membership is a
set the index does not store. It was reported to the author as though it closed
the door on scoping generally; it does not, and that misreading is what this
ticket exists to correct in code.

**A stored attribute is affordable, measured.** `bench/results/0025-year-scope/`
runs each scope both ways over the same items and the same queries on the real
477 512-passage index: an indexed year predicate answers where the `json_each`
blob is 43x slower, and against the same run's unconstrained baseline the filter
is free to within noise — a five-year and a ten-year scope both come out
*cheaper* than no filter, because the predicate removes work.

**The blocker is not latency. The index stores no date.** `items` is
`(item_key, title)`; `passages` carries `(pid, id, item_key, title, text,
source, vector)`. There is no date, no creator, no item type. So three of the
four facets asked for are not slow today — they are impossible. Adding a year
column and its indexes over the whole index cost 529 ms in the probe, and
Zotero's local API already returns `meta.parsedDate` and `data.itemType`, so the
build has the values in hand.

**Upstream takes no filters at all.** `zotero_semantic_search`'s input schema is
`q`, `limit`, `mode`, `auto_build` — nothing else — and its own description
redirects field filtering to `zotero_search_items`, which filters but does not
rank semantically and which measured at seconds per query, unranked
(ticket 0025). So the capability needs an API surface as well as a column.

## Where each facet stands against the sheet
This matters because two of the four are R5 clauses and two are not, and the
difference is the author's to rule on rather than ours to assume.

| facet | in R5's letter? | stored today? |
|---|---|---|
| year / date | **yes** — R5 names date | no |
| item type (doctype) | **yes** — R5 names item type | no |
| author / creator | **no** | no |
| title | **no** | yes, on both `items` and `passages` |

R5 enumerates "collection, tag, item type, or date". Author and title are
outside its letter. Title is already stored and already searchable as text, so
"filter by title" may mean something different from the other three — a field
restriction rather than a scope — and that should be settled before it is built.
Extending R5 to name creator, or filing a sibling requirement, is a ruling for
`DECISIONS.md` and is NOT decided here.

R5's constraint on *how* is already satisfied by the measured shape: the filter
is applied while ranking, so truncation happens after it, which is what R5's
"before any answer is truncated" demands. R5 forbids post-filtering a top-k that
claims completeness; it does not require constraining MATCH, and says so.

## Relevant files
- `bench/year_scope.mjs` — the measurement, both mechanisms, with the control.
- `bench/harvest_years.py` — pulls `meta.parsedDate` from the local API.
- `bench/results/0025-year-scope/year-vs-json-each.json` — the artifact.
- `DECISIONS.md` — carries both awaiting-ratification entries: X4's ladder
  consequence, and the stored-attribute entry this ticket implements.
- `SPEC.md` §3 R5 — the promise; R18 — what an empty scope must say.
- Upstream `src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts` (schema), `build.ts` (where item
  metadata is written), `src/tools/semantic-search.ts` (the input schema).

## Actions
1. **DONE 2026-09-02.** Author ratified the stored-attribute path for
   year/date and item type (DECISIONS.md, "Scoping by a stored attribute is
   affordable..."). Creator and title stay out of this ticket; collections and
   tags stay on X4/0605's mechanism. Nothing left to build for this action —
   do not build a creator filter.
2. **Migration ladder rung.** In `fork/src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts`:
   bump `SCHEMA_VERSION` (currently `2`, :63) to `3` and append a rung to
   `SCHEMA_MIGRATIONS` (currently one entry, `to: 2`, :175-204) whose `up(db)`
   runs, inside the migration's own transaction per the ladder contract
   (:147-157):
   ```
   ALTER TABLE items ADD COLUMN year INTEGER;
   ALTER TABLE items ADD COLUMN item_type TEXT;
   CREATE INDEX items_year ON items(year);
   CREATE INDEX items_item_type ON items(item_type);
   ```
   This is exactly the shape `bench/year_scope.mjs` measured (`ALTER TABLE
   items ADD COLUMN year INTEGER` + `CREATE INDEX items_year ON items(year)`).
   The join side needs no new index: `passages_item` on `passages(item_key)`
   already exists (:939). `createSchema()` (:926-974) also needs the two
   columns/indexes added directly — a fresh install never walks the ladder.
   No vector is touched by this rung (matches the `to: 2` precedent's own
   contract note that most schema changes invalidate no stored row).
3. **Backfill path, and its honest limit.** Extend `putItem(itemKey, title)`
   (abstract at index-manager.ts:416; SQLite impl at sqlite-index.ts:1235-1239,
   backed by `insertItem` at :981) to take `year`/`itemType`, sourced at the
   existing call sites (index-manager.ts:834, :2018) from the same `d =
   item.data` object already destructured there (`d.itemType`; prefer
   `item.meta?.parsedDate` over raw `d.date` if the crawl fetches `meta` —
   confirm this empirically at execution time, not assumed here). `insertItem`
   is `INSERT OR IGNORE` (:981), which never updates an existing row — change
   it to an upsert (`ON CONFLICT(item_key) DO UPDATE SET
   title=excluded.title, year=excluded.year, item_type=excluded.item_type`)
   so a changed item picks up its year/item_type the next time it is touched.
   Incremental updates already delete+reinsert a changed item
   (index-manager.ts:1547, :1582), so this is sufficient for anything the
   crawl revisits. **Items untouched since before the migration keep NULL
   forever until a full rebuild** — this is not an edge case, it is the fact
   Action 6/R18 below has to answer for.
4. **Query-time predicate.** Extend the `keyword` prepared statement
   (sqlite-index.ts:1024-1030) and the vector rescore path
   (`vectorSearch`, :1496) to join `items i ON i.item_key = p.item_key` and add
   `AND i.year BETWEEN ? AND ?` / `AND i.item_type = ?` when a scope is
   supplied — the same join `bench/year_scope.mjs`'s `byYear` statement used,
   measured 43x cheaper than X4's `json_each` mechanism
   (`bench/results/0025-year-scope/year-vs-json-each.json`). Wire it through
   `keywordSearch`/`vectorSearch` (:1422, :1496), called from `query()`
   (index-manager.ts:2087-2136) — the filter must be inside the `pool =
   limit * 3` fetch (line 2094), never a post-filter on `fused` after the
   `hits.length >= limit` truncation at line 2133. This is X4's own mechanism
   distinction (rowid-set join vs. arbitrary rowid blob) and R5's "before any
   answer is truncated" clause.
5. Surface the filter on `zotero_semantic_search`'s input schema
   (src/tools/semantic-search.ts:21-25, currently `q, limit, mode,
   auto_build` only — unchanged at this SHA), as an optional
   `scope: { year?: {from, to}, item_type?: string }`, threaded through
   `ctx.search.query(args.q, {...})` (:115) into `QueryOptions`.
6. Answer R18 honestly for a scoped query: when the ranked stream is exhausted
   before k is filled *because the scope is narrow*, distinguish that from
   "nothing matches" globally. This is the rung that catches what Action 3's
   NULL-until-touched fact creates: an old, unbackfilled item must not read as
   "excluded because out of scope" when it is really "not yet classified".
   X4's verdict made this load-bearing.

## Test
**Unit level, first, red.** Model it on
`fork/tests/features/search-schema-migration.test.ts`, which already builds a
`to: 2`-stamped fixture and upgrades it in-process through the real
`migrationPath()` — its own doc comment names "ADD_COLUMN-style rungs" as
existing generic machinery fixtures, "not shipped migrations"; this ticket
ships the first ADD_COLUMN-shaped rung for real, so it is the natural place to
add cases, not a new test file. Two cases:
1. A `to: 2` fixture with two items of different `year`/`item_type`, migrated
   to `to: 3`. Assert: no vector touched (matches the existing pinned cases in
   that file), `year`/`item_type` columns and their indexes exist, and a query
   scoped to the *non-matching* item's year returns zero hits AND a `scope{}`
   block naming the reason — not an empty list. This fails today for the
   strongest possible reason: no filter argument exists to write, and no
   `year`/`item_type` column exists to filter on.
2. A pre-migration item (present before the `to: 3` rung ran, so `year`/
   `item_type` are NULL after migration) is excluded from a year-scoped query
   *with the "not yet classified" R18 distinction*, not silently matched and
   not silently folded into "nothing matches" — this is Action 3's backfill
   limit made concrete, and it is a case a real-corpus run cannot exercise
   deterministically the way a fixture can (which items are unbackfilled on
   the real library depends on incremental-update history, not on this rung).

Small, in-memory or tmp-file SQLite, no network, no embedder — fast, part of
`npm test` (vitest) in `fork/`.

**Deferred, named, non-blocking: real-corpus validation.** Against the running
keyword-only build at `bench/results/0605-padme-full-index/` (sqlite, ~7,546
items, no embeddings) once it is available — as of this Plan pass its
`build-result.json` already shows a *completed* run (elapsed_s 142.5, 7546
items, 481920 documents), which may mean the "many hours out" framing at
kickoff was about a different, still-running process; confirm the actual
process state before treating it as free capacity. Either way this is Phase 5
execution's job: do not start a competing build or a heavy local-API-driving
test against padme's Zotero desktop while anything is still using it.

## Verification
- [ ] Migration rung `to: 3` upgrades a `to: 2` fixture in place: no re-embed,
      no vector touched, `year`/`item_type` columns and their indexes exist
      afterward (unit, fixture scale)
- [ ] A fresh install (`createSchema()` path) carries the same columns/indexes
      without ever walking the ladder (unit)
- [ ] A year-scoped and an item-type-scoped query each return only matching
      items, on a fixture (unit, first) and later on the real library (follow-up)
- [ ] The filter is applied before truncation, shown by a case where an
      unscoped top-k contains none of the in-scope hits (unit, fixture scale;
      re-check on the real index in the follow-up)
- [ ] A pre-migration, unbackfilled item is excluded from a scoped query with
      the "not yet classified" distinction, not silently matched and not
      folded into "nothing matches" (unit)
- [ ] An empty scoped result (scope narrow, corpus has nothing in it) carries
      R18's distinction from an unbackfilled item's exclusion above — two
      different reasons, two different messages
- [ ] p95 for a scoped query stays within the unscoped baseline's band on the
      real index — follow-up, once the real-corpus run is available; the probe
      already measured no marginal cost at SQL level, this re-checks it in the
      server
- [ ] `npm test` (vitest) green in `fork/`; `make check` green in this repo

## Invariants
- X4's verdict stands: no `json_each` rowid-set step ships. This ticket adds a
  different mechanism, it does not retry that one.
- R5's "before any answer is truncated" is not weakened to a post-filter.
- Coverage honesty (R4, R17) survives: a scoped answer must not read as complete
  when the scope is only partly indexed.
- Nothing here is filed upstream without the author's authorization.

## Exit criteria
- [x] The author/title reading is ruled on in `DECISIONS.md` — done 2026-09-02
- [ ] Year and item-type scoping work end to end against the real library
- [ ] R18's scope block answers an exhausted scope, and distinguishes it from
      an unbackfilled (pre-migration) item's exclusion
- [ ] The stored-attribute ledger entry is ratified and SPEC.md §5.2.2 edited to match
